1. Nf3 {psychology was a huge factor in this game. going into it I was intimidated! playing a 13 year old prodigy with a 2260 FIDE rating. I sought to avoid complications and play as positionally as possible, avoiding the e4 break which was what my intuition and the position demanded in favour of a slower maneuvering game. Ultimately I was overthinking everything, telling mhyself a single innacuracy would result in death. And burning lots of clock in the opening and getting nervous. } d5 2. d4 Nf6 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3  (4. Bf4  ) Be7 5. Bf4 b6 6. cxd5 exd5 7. Qc2  (7. e3  Nh5 8. Be5 Nd7  (8... f6  9. Bxc7 Qxc7)) c5 8. g3  (8. e3  Nh5 9. Bxb8 Rxb8 10. Ne5) Nc6 9. Bg2 O-O 10. O-O h6 11. Rfd1 Be6 12. Nb5?! {completely missing that the c file will open and I lose a tempo after rc8} Rc8 {[%cal Gc5d4]} 13. a3  (13. Ne5 {this is what I saw before hand}  ) a6 14. Nc3 c4 15. Ne5 Na5 16. Re1 b5 17. Rad1 Re8 18. h3 {this plan is too slow. I was scared of e4 because I was nervous playing a 2260 rated kid! but the position demanded e4, all my pieces were in the right place for this break. What I didn't like about it was that If I took on d5, he could set up a blockade against my IQP. But I could have just played e4 and then sat with the tension, opened the position up at my leasure. Instead this KIA style pawn storm was way too slow and does nothing. }  (18. e4! {I thought this wasn’t good because I thought he could just ignore it and if I take set up a blockade. I was thinking purely positionally and not tactically. Overall lessons from this game and tournament. Don't be scared of pawn breaks! throughout the tournament I was scared of executing pawn breaks that my intuition was screaming for. }  dxe4  (18... Nb3  19. exd5 Nxd5 {this is what I saw and stopped at. I just assumed black would set up an easy blockade and was better. But I completely missed a tactic. } 20. Bxh6!! {sacrificing EVERYTHING to open up the king. insanity}  (20. Nxd5  Bxd5 21. Qf5 {[%cal Ge5g4]there are other ways to play this positionally and still retain an advantage. Yes black has a blockade on my d pawn but I have a lot of active threats} c3 {[%cal Gg2e4]}  (21... Bf6  22. Ng4 Bxg2 23. Rxe8+ Qxe8 24. Nxf6+ gxf6 25. Qg4+  (25. Kxg2  Qe6) Kh8 26. Kxg2)  (21... Bxg2  )  (21... Bf8  22. Be4! {the key idea is to overload the bishop on d5. } g6 23. Nxg6 Bxe4 24. Rxe4 Rxe4 25. Qxe4)) gxh6  (20... f6  ) 21. Nxf7 Bxf7 22. Bxd5 Bxd5 23. Qg6+ Kh8 24. Qxh6+ Kg8 25. Qg6+ Kf8 26. Re5 {now this line is extremely difficult to see. })  (18... Nc6 {and if black doesn't take andf just sits with the tension? then that's completely fine, sit with the tension of the pawn break and improve your position with moves like h4. }  19. h4)  (18... Bf8  19. exd5 Nxd5 20. Nxf7 Bxf7 21. Rxe8 Qxe8 22. Nxd5 {there's even this beautiful deflection tactic in the air if black moves his dark squared bishop away. })  (18... Qb6  ) 19. d5  (19. Nxe4  ) Bxd5  (19... Bf5  20. d6 Bf8 21. Nxe4) 20. Nxd5 Nxd5) Bf8  (18... Qb6  19. Be3 Nc6  (19... Rcd8  20. f4 Nc6)) 19. Be3 Qb6 20. f4 Rcd8  (20... Nc6  21. f5) 21. f5 {too weak and too slow. playing for e4 here was the much beetter plan. or jexpanding with g5 before commiting to f5. }  (21. Bf2  )  (21. g4!  ) Bc8 22. Bf2 Nc6 23. g4 a5 24. Qd2  (24. e4  b4 25. Nxd5 Nxd5 26. exd5) b4 25. Nb1 Nxe5 26. dxe5 Bc5 27. exf6  (27. Bxc5  Qxc5+ 28. Qd4 Qxd4+ 29. Rxd4 Rxe5 30. Bf3) Bxf2+ 28. Kh1 Bxe1 29. Qxe1 Qxf6 30. Bxd5 Rxd5 31. Rxd5 Bb7 *

